ame as keops  the pharaohs associated with the smaller  pyramids at giza are kafrei and menkauri  and they're all successive rulers the  fourth dynasty of the old kingdom  although we know very little about khufu  we know he was born at the end of the  26th century bce  and lived more than 26 years  he was the son of king sneferu the  greatest pyramid builder of all time  the only named portrait of khufu is a  small ivory statuette just three inches  high  the irony is striking  khufu is often portrayed negatively in  ancient texts the westcard papyrus a  collection of stories written in the  late middle kingdom describes khufu as  heartless and inquisitive  in the papyrus khufu's sons speak in  turn telling tales for their father's  amusement  the fourth story tells of a magician  named daddy who has the power to  reattach an animal severed head to its  body among other things  daddy performs his magic successfully  before the king  khufu then decides to test the magician  and brings a condemned prisoner to be  decapitated  daddy is outraged  it is against the god's will  the fifth century bce greek historian  herodotus wrote that kiops the greek  name for khufu was a tyrant who enslaved  his people and the first century ce  jewish historian josephus claimed the  hebrews built the pyramids yet the  largest pyramids were constructed over a  thousand years before the hebrews  arrived in egypt still this idea  persists in popular imagination  despite khufu's bad press  the ancient egyptians revered him  the egyptians celebrated king khufu's  cult nearly 2 000 years after his death  some egyptologists suggest khufu saw  himself as the incarnation of the sun  god  ra  khufu's son king jedif ray called  himself the son of ra  the great pyramid is awesome in many  ways  part of this is a spectacular feat of  engineering  part of this is that the square base of  the pyramid has its edges accurately  aligned north south and east west  that is the alignment is cardinal  the 